{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794144967,"nonce":"lIMfLj832A9cw6nsoAuMHaJ4SQQ=","title":"apple::font_path returns a substitute font for unknown family names, so the registry loads Helvetica.ttc once per name","message":"`apple::font_path(name)` calls `CTFontCreateWithName`, which answers an unknown name with a substitute (Helvetica), and returns that file's path. `FontRegistry` then loads it into the database and finds nothing under the requested name. On an iPhone 12 Pro Max, \"Noto Sans\", \"Noto Sans Symbols\", \"Noto Sans Symbols2\", \"FreeSans\", \"DejaVu Sans\" and \"Blinc Noto Symbols\" each loaded `/System/Library/Fonts/Core/Helvetica.ttc` (10 loads in one run, found afterwards: false).\n\nNot a per-frame cost, but it adds duplicate faces to the database and costs a few ms at startup. Accept the path only when the returned font's family or PostScript name matches the request (CTFontCopyFamilyName / CTFontCopyPostScriptName), or compare against the system UI aliases. The CoreText glyph path added for hvgl faces already guards the same hazard by comparing glyph counts.","files":null}]}